\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Geometry-only views of the config-4 WLS-fibre clamp, rendered from the Geant4\ VRML geometry export (\emph  {no} beam-on). Following the event-display colour scheme, fibre cores are pale green, cladding dark green, the grease film amber, the black clamp and holder dark grey, and the read-out SiPM red. The four \SI {1.0}{\milli \meter } fibres sit on the $-x$ narrow face in \SI {1.1}{\milli \meter } channels separated by clamp webs, with only a thin grease film in the slab--clamp gap; they converge to a bundle read out at $-y$ by one SiPM and mirrored at $+y$. These screenshots show the clamp in its earlier black visualisation colour; the production clamp is white (Fig.~\ref {fig:wlsogl}).}}{11}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:wlslayout}{{2}{11}{Geometry-only views of the config-4 WLS-fibre clamp, rendered from the \gfour \ VRML geometry export (\emph {no} beam-on). Following the event-display colour scheme, fibre cores are pale green, cladding dark green, the grease film amber, the black clamp and holder dark grey, and the read-out SiPM red. The four \SI {1.0}{\milli \meter } fibres sit on the $-x$ narrow face in \SI {1.1}{\milli \meter } channels separated by clamp webs, with only a thin grease film in the slab--clamp gap; they converge to a bundle read out at $-y$ by one SiPM and mirrored at $+y$. These screenshots show the clamp in its earlier black visualisation colour; the production clamp is white (Fig.~\ref {fig:wlsogl})}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces OpenGL (\texttt  {TSG\_OFFSCREEN}) geometry renders of the config-4 assembly (multi-clad $n = 1.59/1.49/1.42$ fibres, white clamp), generated head-lessly from Geant4 (no beam-on, drawn without wireframe edges). Panels~(a) and~(b) use the \emph  {tessellated} visualisation preset (\texttt  {macros/muon\_vis\_wls\_ogl.mac}): it is geometrically equivalent to the production analytic preset---identical round \SI {1.0}{\milli \meter } fibres and clamp/grease volumes---but its clamp and grease film render as solids, whereas the analytic cut-tube cutters do not implement \texttt  {CreatePolyhedron()} and would appear as point clouds. Panel~(c) instead uses the \emph  {analytic production} geometry (\texttt  {macros/muon\_vis\_wls\_ogl\_coupling.mac}) with only those two Boolean solids hidden, so that the plain \texttt  {G4CutTubs} fibres, the read-out pad and the SiPM are seen coupling. These renders complement the hand-made VRML screenshots of Fig.~\ref {fig:wlslayout}.}}{12}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:wlsogl}{{3}{12}{OpenGL (\texttt {TSG\_OFFSCREEN}) geometry renders of the config-4 assembly (multi-clad $n = 1.59/1.49/1.42$ fibres, white clamp), generated head-lessly from Geant4 (no beam-on, drawn without wireframe edges). Panels~(a) and~(b) use the \emph {tessellated} visualisation preset (\texttt {macros/muon\_vis\_wls\_ogl.mac}): it is geometrically equivalent to the production analytic preset---identical round \SI {1.0}{\milli \meter } fibres and clamp/grease volumes---but its clamp and grease film render as solids, whereas the analytic cut-tube cutters do not implement \texttt {CreatePolyhedron()} and would appear as point clouds. Panel~(c) instead uses the \emph {analytic production} geometry (\texttt {macros/muon\_vis\_wls\_ogl\_coupling.mac}) with only those two Boolean solids hidden, so that the plain \texttt {G4CutTubs} fibres, the read-out pad and the SiPM are seen coupling. These renders complement the hand-made VRML screenshots of Fig.~\ref {fig:wlslayout}}{figure.caption.5}{}}
