\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Head-less (\texttt  {TSG\_OFFSCREEN}) event display of one vertical muon in the glossy-wrapped plate with the config-1 layout, on a white background. The red line is the muon and the blue lines are optical photons (here purely scintillation, since the muon is below the \v {C}erenkov threshold); the plate and its reflective wrapper are drawn as translucent surfaces with their edges outlined, and the SiPMs are opaque red. Both the muon energy (\SI {30}{\mega \electronvolt }, below the \v {C}erenkov threshold) and the photon yield are chosen for visualisation only, so that individual tracks are resolved rather than merging into a saturated blob. The display is generated reproducibly by \texttt  {macros/muon\_vis.mac}.}}{18}{figure.caption.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:vis}{{8}{18}{Head-less (\texttt {TSG\_OFFSCREEN}) event display of one vertical muon in the glossy-wrapped plate with the config-1 layout, on a white background. The red line is the muon and the blue lines are optical photons (here purely scintillation, since the muon is below the \v {C}erenkov threshold); the plate and its reflective wrapper are drawn as translucent surfaces with their edges outlined, and the SiPMs are opaque red. Both the muon energy (\SI {30}{\mega \electronvolt }, below the \v {C}erenkov threshold) and the photon yield are chosen for visualisation only, so that individual tracks are resolved rather than merging into a saturated blob. The display is generated reproducibly by \texttt {macros/muon\_vis.mac}}{figure.caption.11}{}}
