\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Photoelectron spectra in the layout of Fig.~10 of Rao et al.~\cite  {Rao2025}: rows are the irradiation regions (centre, edge, corner) and columns the detector configurations (1--4, with the WLS-clamp layout as the fourth column). Each cell overlays the four reflector treatments. The histograms are normalised entry distributions (each cell is scaled so its entries sum to unity). The \emph  {upper} grid~(a) uses linear axes and the \emph  {lower} grid~(b) logarithmic axes; both share the same reflector colours and styles and the same units. Panel~(a) gives each column its own linear abscissa, truncated at that configuration's 99.5th percentile (rounded up) and binned linearly, so that the narrow high-density core of every layout is resolved. A single shared linear abscissa would instead collapse the WLS-clamp layout (config~4), which collects roughly an order of magnitude fewer photoelectrons than the direct-coupled layouts, into the first few percent of the axis. Panel~(b) shows every cell over its full distribution on a single shared logarithmic abscissa with logarithmic bins, so the four layouts remain directly comparable there. Zero-photoelectron events form a pulse below threshold and lie off the abscissa. The wrappers shift and broaden the spectrum from the low, contact-limited bare distribution through TiO$_2$ and air-backed Al to the wide, bright glossy one; apart from TiO$_2$ and the bare plate the spectra vary by at most \SI {3.4}{\percent } across positions.}}{29}{figure.caption.23}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pegrid}{{16}{29}{Photoelectron spectra in the layout of Fig.~10 of Rao et al.~\cite {Rao2025}: rows are the irradiation regions (centre, edge, corner) and columns the detector configurations (1--4, with the WLS-clamp layout as the fourth column). Each cell overlays the four reflector treatments. The histograms are normalised entry distributions (each cell is scaled so its entries sum to unity). The \emph {upper} grid~(a) uses linear axes and the \emph {lower} grid~(b) logarithmic axes; both share the same reflector colours and styles and the same units. Panel~(a) gives each column its own linear abscissa, truncated at that configuration's 99.5th percentile (rounded up) and binned linearly, so that the narrow high-density core of every layout is resolved. A single shared linear abscissa would instead collapse the WLS-clamp layout (config~4), which collects roughly an order of magnitude fewer photoelectrons than the direct-coupled layouts, into the first few percent of the axis. Panel~(b) shows every cell over its full distribution on a single shared logarithmic abscissa with logarithmic bins, so the four layouts remain directly comparable there. Zero-photoelectron events form a pulse below threshold and lie off the abscissa. The wrappers shift and broaden the spectrum from the low, contact-limited bare distribution through TiO$_2$ and air-backed Al to the wide, bright glossy one; apart from TiO$_2$ and the bare plate the spectra vary by at most \SI {3.4}{\percent } across positions}{figure.caption.23}{}}
